text:
Ok, I guess I have karma to burn for the inevitable downvotes..<p>&quot;I&#x27;ve&quot; been writing a compiler. It&#x27;s pretty good - comparable to C++&#x2F;gcc&#x2F;clang in speed and has some (IMHO) nice features, amongst which:<p>- it doesn&#x27;t use header files, for library imports it reads the DWARF to get method&#x2F;funciton signatures and types<p>- it has the nicest interface I&#x27;ve ever seen for a GPU&#x2F;CPU interconnect, just one line of code identifies the bit to convert to a GPU kernel, and I&#x27;m working on getting rid of that too.<p>- the same language is used for the GPU and CPU, so the below is a full and complete GPU-driven Mandelbrot program. On my M4 Max MBP it operates in 0.7ms on the GPU, 94ms on all-cores (another option it can do) or 432ms on a single core.<p><pre><code>     #use &quot;Stdio.xc&quot;
     #use &quot;Par.xc&quot;

     #define W 2048

     i32 main(void)
     {
         u32 total = 0;
         par mandel :reduce(+ total)
             {
             for (u32 i in 0..W * W)
                 {
                 float cx = (float)(i % W) * (3.0f &#x2F; W) - 2.0f;
                 float cy = (float)(i &#x2F; W) * (3.0f &#x2F; W) - 1.5f;
                 float x = 0.0f;
                 float y = 0.0f;
                 u32 k = 0;
                 while (k &lt; 256 &amp;&amp; x * x + y * y &lt; 4.0f)
                     {
                     float xt = x * x - y * y + cx;
                     y = 2.0f * x * y + cy;
                     x = xt;
                     k = k + 1;
                     }
                 total = total + k;
                 }
             }
         printf(&quot;%u iterations\n&quot;, total);
         return 0;
     }
</code></pre>
- The &#x27;par&#x27; line is the only thing that is a bit different from a &#x27;C&#x27;-like language, all the data-hazards between CPU&#x2F;GPU are taken care of, and there&#x27;s no setup boilerplate. xc will compile the above straight out of the box.<p>- Currently working on automatic division of labour between any GPUs on the same machine, initial results are promising, and then onwards to GPUs on the LAN.<p>- Binding of the GPU happens at runtime, not compile-time, meaning the best possible execution path will be chosen (by measuring, then persisting the measurement so it doesn&#x27;t have to run again for the same parameters) on any given machine, be that an NVIDIA, AMD, or Apple platform, or a WGSL-based browser.<p>Anyway. Brief synopsis over with. The point is that a lot of the above things are my own ideas, there isn&#x27;t really anything out there (that I know of) that parses DWARF for data. There&#x27;s no language out there (that I know of) that has such a clean interface between CPU and GPU, Runtime measurement and efficiency management built in, with persistence, complete independence from vendor tools. Etc. etc.<p>This isn&#x27;t just a &quot;hey Claude, build me a compiler, I want it to be rad, yo&quot; minimal-effort thing - I&#x27;ve spent more than half a year doing this, there&#x27;s been a lot of effort involved, steering, redirecting when things don&#x27;t pan out, discussing, overrriding the recommended path in the name of future goals, etc.<p>Don&#x27;t get me wrong, I&#x27;ve written compilers before, <i>literal</i> decades ago (I&#x27;m retired now), so I know just what a huge job it was to take on, and I&#x27;d never have attempted it myself if the LLM wasn&#x27;t there to do the grunt work, but there&#x27;s still a lot of &quot;me&quot; in this. My vision. My direction. My time.<p>What I have previously done is say &quot;I&quot; wrote the compiler (though that&#x27;s been too subtle in the past) so I now tend to say &quot;I wrote a compiler (full disclosure: with Claude)&quot;. And then I ignore the people who get upset about the LLM thing, you know, like water off a duck&#x27;s back.

There&#x27;s also this:<p><pre><code>    &gt; &quot;The ULEZ camera had already been cut down&quot;
</code></pre>
The camera was already useless. Detonating an IED served no purposeful protest, and endangered property and lives.
